  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe 500 hPa closed low will continue to fill in as it slowly gets \nabsorbed back into the main storm track through the first half of \nthe work week. CAM guidance (and more specifically the HRRR runs \nsince this afternoon) continue to show the next wave of \nshowers/thunderstorms forming off the surface trough analyzed across \nalong the Rio Grande. The HRRR continues to show 1-3 inches \ngenerally from Duval County toward an area between Corpus Christi \nand Baffin Bay. Forecast rainfall totals greater than 4 inches \nthrough Monday afternoon generally will stay closest to the \nimmediate coastline, though any training storm, or stronger \nconvective storm, has the ability to produce these amounts. Rainfall \nover the last 24 hrs across the aforementioned area ranged from 5 to \nnear 10 inches of rain. Needless to say, soils are saturated. With 2-\n4 inch rainfall rates still in the forecast, it will not take long \nto get quick responses from small creeks and streams in the area. \nThe latest 12 hr Rapid Onset Flooding hot spots highlight this area \nanalyzed by the NWC (see Area Hydrologic Discussion #26), so things \nare primed for yet another messy morning commute. Many of the local \nschool districts and local governments offices have already delayed \nopenings given the higher risk for flash flooding, so delaying your \ncommute, if able, is highly encouraged. For this reason, the WPC is \ncontinuing to place portions of this region under a Slight Risk for \nFlash Flooding (level 2 of 4).\n\nAt the upper levels, a subtropical jet streak redevelops downstream \nof the next trough moving into the WCONUS today. These jet dynamics \nwill continue to bring enhanced vertical motion across South Texas \nwhile a surface boundary near the I-10 corridor remains draped \nacross the area, resulting in continuing rain chances (20-40%) \nthrough mid-week. \n\nBy Wednesday night, the next cold front will move through, with the \ninitial wind shift early Thursday morning and the reinforcing high \npressure building in later Thursday morning. This will usher in \nlower dewpoints (low to mid-60s on Wednesday down to upper 40s/50s \nby Thursday night). While daytime temperatures will generally \ncontinue climb to the low to mid 70s, morning low temperatures \nFriday and Saturday morning will drop into the upper 40s in the \nVictoria Crossroads and across the northern Brush Country. By the \nend of the weekend, onshore flow returns, bringing back Gulf \nmoisture and regulating temperatures to near-normal values by the \nupcoming week. While confidence in rain prospects for the end of the \nforecast is low, a similar upper-level pattern of a low closing off \nfrom the storm track over northwestern Mexico will place Texas back \ninto a diffluent pattern resulting in increased rain chances for the \nupcoming weekend, but we'll see how deep this trough digs into that \nregion before placing increased confidence on rainfall amounts.\n\nAcross our Gulf-facing beaches, the high rip current risk continues \nthrough this evening before becoming more of a moderate risk tonight \nand a low risk by Tuesday. Minor coastal flooding associated water \nlevels approaching the 2.0 ft above MSL mark will continue through \ntoday's high tide cycle, with the overall coastal flooding risk \ndecreasing through the latter half of the week.",
